{"id": "4b0cba73-6037-40cb-895c-56870f75cf21", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_AKTZrFWY9HfD4iI5kGiVDj", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-api-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Produce a complete audit of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key in AWS account 123456789012 (the only associated account) and recommend which to remove. READ-ONLY: do not make any mutating calls.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName 'bedrock.amazonaws.com', attached to IAM users (often users auto-created with names like 'BedrockAPIKey-xxxx' and the managed policy AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess, but any IAM user may own one). The user wants a full report and a clear remove / keep / review recommendation per key. Short-term keys (presigned, up to 12h) are not in scope but mention if observed.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 123456789012", "IAM (global, use us-east-1)", "CloudTrail (us-east-1 plus any region where Bedrock calls appear)"],
  "timeframe": "Last 90 days for usage (use datetime tool for exact ISO timestamps; CloudTrail lookup may only cover 90 days)",
  "criteria": "1) Enumerate ALL IAM users (paginate) and call iam list_service_specific_credentials for each (filter ServiceName=bedrock.amazonaws.com; also note any other service-specific credential types separately). For each key capture: ServiceSpecificCredentialId, ServiceUserName, owning IAM user, Status (Active/Inactive), CreateDate, ExpirationDate (or no expiry), age in days. 2) For each owning user capture: attached managed policies, inline policies, group memberships and their policies, permissions boundary, whether the user has console password / access keys / MFA, tags, user's PasswordLastUsed, and flag overly broad permissions (e.g. AdministratorAccess, bedrock:*, iam:*, wildcard resources, AmazonBedrockFullAccess vs AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess). 3) Determine last usage / recent activity per key or user via CloudTrail (lookup_cloudtrail_events — Bedrock/bedrock-runtime events by that user, CreateServiceSpecificCredential / ResetServiceSpecificCredential / UpdateServiceSpecificCredential events and who created them and when). 4) Note account-level context if cheaply available: any SCP/IAM conditions restricting long-term keys, and AWS guidance that long-term Bedrock keys are for exploration only and short-term keys are recommended for production (verify via docs). 5) Classify each key as REMOVE / REVIEW / KEEP with a short reason (e.g. inactive status, no usage in 90 days, no expiry, older than 90 days, overly broad permissions, user also has other credentials, owned by a non-dedicated user). Return: a summary count, a per-key table, per-key rationale, and general hardening recommendations. If zero keys exist, state clearly that none were found and how thoroughly you checked (number of users scanned). Report any permission errors encountered so gaps are visible."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit Bedrock long-term API keys across account IAM users"}], "created_at": null}

